<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Keeping Our Communities Clean – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> We Should Clean the Classroom on Friday.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: reading_1: Interpret simple charts and graphs related to texts.<br>reading_2: Analyze characters' actions and motives in stories.<br>phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: Can interpret data in charts about community activities (e.g., 'Most students like to clean the garden.').<br>Can analyze actions and motives of characters in short stories about supporting the community (e.g., 'She is happy because she will participate in the clean-up campaign.').<br>Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ɪ/ in 'bring' and /iː/ in 'clean').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f0f0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of a dirty classroom and a clean classroom. The teacher asks, "Which classroom do you like? Why?" and "What things do we need to clean?" (e.g., "bags," "tools," "supplies"). Students brainstorm words from Lesson 1 (e.g., "clean," "trash," "recycling").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple bar chart on the board or projector titled "Our Classroom Cleaning Tasks." The teacher models how to read the chart by pointing and saying, "This chart shows who will help clean. Look, 'picking up trash' is done by 10 students. 'Wiping desks' is done by 8 students." The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "clean," "bring," "trash," "should," "will." The teacher emphasizes word stress on imperatives like 'bring' and 'clean'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does this chart tell us?" (Checks understanding of chart purpose)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'clean the board', am I asking or telling?" (Checks understanding of imperative function and word stress)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'clean' a long sound or a short sound?" (Checks phonemic awareness for /iː/)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text titled "Our Class Clean-up Day" describing a class's plan to clean the classroom on Friday, incorporating characters, their tasks, and feelings. The text includes an embedded simple bar chart showing the "Favorite Cleaning Tasks" of the students (e.g., "Sweep the floor," "Wipe desks," "Organize books," "Collect recycling"). The text uses vocabulary such as "community," "tasks," "group," "project," "bags," "recycling," "volunteers," "team," "responsibility," and verbs like "clean," "pick up," "collect," "organize," "sort," "recycle," and modal verbs "should," "can," "will."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information and general understanding. Interpreting simple visual data (charts) within a text. Analyzing characters' actions and motives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher distributes the text. Students read the story individually.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main idea of the story?" "When will the class clean?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements and mark them True or False based on the text (e.g., "The class will clean on Saturday." "Maria is sad about the clean-up.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match characters from the story (e.g., "Leo," "Maria," "Carlos") to their specific cleaning tasks mentioned in the text (e.g., "collect recycling," "wipe desks," "sweep the floor").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpreting written information, including charts. Analyzing characters' actions and motives. Identifying modal verbs (should, can, must), future tense (will), and imperatives. Reinforcing vocabulary related to community clean-up. Practicing phonemic awareness with short and long vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Reading for Detail):</b> Students re-read the story and underline all sentences containing modal verbs (`should`, `can`, `must`) and future tense (`will`). Then, they complete cloze sentences using words from the story's vocabulary (e.g., "The students will _______ the classroom on Friday." "We ________ pick up the trash." "Leo is a helpful _______."). Teacher guides students to identify short vowel /ɪ/ in 'bring' and long vowel /iː/ in 'clean' from the text as they read.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Chart Interpretation & Character Analysis):</b> Students look at the embedded bar chart from the story. They answer questions such as: "According to the chart, which task do most students like?" "How many students prefer to 'collect recycling'?" Then, students answer open-ended questions about character motives: "Why is Maria happy?" (e.g., "She is happy because she will participate in the clean-up campaign."). "What can Leo do to help?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Peer Check & Discussion):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from Activity 1 and 2. They read aloud the sentences they underlined and the answers to the chart questions. They discuss their analysis of character motives, referring back to specific parts of the text (e.g., "I think Maria is happy because the text says 'Maria smiled, excited to help her team.'"). The teacher monitors and assists with pronunciation, especially word stress on imperatives and vowel sounds.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides a new, short reading text: "Our School Garden Project Plan." This text describes a different community project (cleaning the school garden) and includes a simple bar chart showing "Tools Needed" for the project (e.g., "Gloves," "Shovels," "Watering Cans"). The text uses vocabulary like "project," "volunteers," "tools," "area," "environment," and grammar structures such as "We will bring..." and "Volunteers must wear..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Reading and Interpreting a School Garden Project Plan with a 'Tools Needed' chart."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work individually to read the new text and answer questions focused on reading comprehension and chart interpretation:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main goal of this project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the chart, which tool is most needed?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What must volunteers wear?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who will organize the tools?" (analyzing character actions/roles from the text)</li>
                </ul>
                Students then briefly explain their answers to a partner, verbally describing the information they read from the text and the chart.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during pair discussion and review of written answers. Focus on correct interpretation of text and charts, and analysis of character actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short worksheet with a simplified version of the "Our School Garden Project Plan" text and chart, answering 3-4 comprehension questions and one question requiring character motive analysis (e.g., "Why is Mr. Lopez described as 'responsible' in the plan?"). Students also identify two words with a short vowel sound and two words with a long vowel sound from the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly interprets data in the chart (e.g., identifies most/least needed tools).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Analyzes characters' actions and motives accurately based on the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary from the lesson in answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies short and long vowel sounds correctly from the text.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned about reading charts is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now understand why characters act a certain way by reading the text. Yes/No/Sometimes."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the stories or charts was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging, and how can I improve my reading?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., student engagement with the story, chart interpretation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially for students who struggled with chart interpretation or character analysis? (e.g., more visual aids, simpler texts, explicit modeling of inference).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: The vocabulary and theme from this reading lesson will be directly used in the next lesson, which focuses on speaking and interaction about planning community clean-up tasks.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>